  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "With an array of strings `nums` containing `n` **unique** binary strings each of length `n`, output *a binary string of length* `n` *that **does not appear** in* `nums`*. If there are multiple answers, you may output **any** of them*.\n\n**Example 1:**\n\n```\nInput: nums = [\"01\",\"10\"]\nOutput: \"11\"\nExplanation: \"11\" does not appear in nums. \"00\" would also be correct.\n```\n\n**Example 2:**\n\n```\nInput: nums = [\"00\",\"01\"]\nOutput: \"11\"\nExplanation: \"11\" does not appear in nums. \"10\" would also be correct.\n```\n\n**Example 3:**\n\n```\nInput: nums = [\"111\",\"011\",\"001\"]\nOutput: \"101\"\nExplanation: \"101\" does not appear in nums. \"000\", \"010\", \"100\", and \"110\" would also be correct.\n```\n\n**Constraints:**\n\n* `n == nums.length`\n* `1 <= n <= 16`\n* `nums[i].length == n`\n* `nums[i]` is either `'0'` or `'1'`.\n* All the strings of `nums` are **unique**.",
    "constraints": [
      "None"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Single line: JSON array of all arguments, e.g. [arg1, arg2, ...]",
    "outputFormat": "Single line: JSON-serialised return value",
